---
import Button from"../../components/ui/Button.astro";

import MedicalNote from"../../components/ui/MedicalNote.astro";
import ContentLayout from"../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import type { FaqItem } from"../../lib/seo";
import ArticleImage from"../../components/ui/ArticleImage.astro";

const faqs: FaqItem[] = [
  { q:"How do I gain weight if I'm a hard gainer?", a:"Increase calories gradually, prioritize calorie-dense foods, eat more frequently, and lift weights. If you're not gaining after two weeks, add another 200–300 calories per day." },
  { q:"How fast should I gain weight?", a:"For lean gains, aim for 0.25–0.5 kg (0.5–1 lb) per week. Faster gains add more fat relative to muscle." },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Weight Gain Guide — How to Gain Weight & Build Muscle"
  h1="The Complete Weight Gain Guide"
  description="How to gain weight and build muscle the healthy way: setting a calorie surplus, eating enough protein, and training effectively."
  path="/guides/weight-gain-guide/"
  category="Guides"
  categoryPath="/guides/"
  lead="Gaining weight is harder than it looks for some. Here's how to add quality mass — mostly muscle, not just fat."
  updated="January 2025"
  faqs={faqs}
>

  <MedicalNote />

  <ArticleImage src="/assets/images/articles/muscle-growth.svg" alt="Illustration detailing the process of healthy weight gain and muscle hypertrophy." />

  <div class="prose-content">
    <p class="text-lg md:text-xl text-body leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Although the majority of media, social networks, and the fitness industry focus aggressively on weight loss, a very significant portion of the population faces a diametrically opposite biological and behavioral challenge: <strong>gaining weight healthily and consistently</strong>. Whether you are a high-performance athlete looking to increase muscle mass to improve strength and aesthetics, a person with a genetically fast metabolism (a"hardgainer") who can't seem to make the scale move, or an individual recovering from an illness needing to restore a healthy Body Mass Index (BMI), this definitive and science-backed guide covers in-depth how to add lean mass to your frame without accumulating unnecessary and dangerous body fat.
    </p>

    <div class="bg-gradient-to-br from-surface to-canvas-soft/50 rounded-3xl p-8 mb-12 shadow-sm border border-hairline/50 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute top-0 right-0 w-32 h-32 bg-blue-200/40 rounded-full blur-3xl -mr-10 -mt-10"></div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-ink mb-4 relative z-10">Why is it so difficult for some people to gain weight?</h2>
      <p class="text-body relative z-10 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        For most, the solution to being unable to gain weight seems painfully simple:"just eat more." However, the reality of human biology and satiety mechanisms is far more intricate. Those who persistently struggle to put on weight often possess a highly adaptive biological mechanism called <strong>Non-Exercise Activity Thermogenesis (NEAT)</strong>. This means that, in response to an increase in caloric intake, their body subconsciously begins to burn a massive amount of extra calories through tiny imperceptible movements (like constantly tapping a foot, repeatedly shifting posture, gesturing expansively while speaking, or pacing back and forth).
      </p>
      <p class="text-body relative z-10 leading-relaxed">
        Furthermore, these individuals usually have hormone-mediated satiety signals (such as leptin and peptide YY) that trigger extremely quickly. Their brain strongly signals them to stop eating long before they have reached a caloric surplus. Gaining weight, therefore, is not simply an act of sheer willpower at the dining table; it requires a calculated and scientifically backed strategy to force the body to build new tissue, optimizing muscle hypertrophy and minimizing adiposity.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-ink mt-12 mb-6">The Physiology of Weight Gain: The Caloric Surplus Explained</h2>
    <p class="text-body leading-relaxed mb-4">
      The universal laws of thermodynamics invariably dictate changes in human body mass. Just as weight loss requires sustaining a prolonged energy deficit, physical weight gain demands the opposite state: a <strong>caloric surplus</strong>. In simple terms, you must systematically and consistently consume more calories than your body expends in a 24-hour period (your Total Daily Energy Expenditure or TDEE).
    </p>
    <p class="text-body leading-relaxed mb-6">
      However, the main goal should never be to simply"make the needle on the scale go up" by gaining pure adipose tissue (fat). The clinical and aesthetic purpose should focus on gaining functional, lean muscle mass, with a minimal accompanying accumulation of necessary fat. This is achieved through careful modulation of the magnitude of the caloric surplus and the presence of an intense mechanical stimulus through training.
    </p>
    
    <div class="my-10 bg-surface/90 backdrop-blur-md rounded-3xl border border-hairline/80 p-8 md:p-12 shadow-[0_20px_50px_-12px_rgba(0,0,0,0.1)] relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute -top-4 -left-4 w-24 h-24 bg-surface rounded-full blur-2xl opacity-70"></div>
      <h3 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-ink mb-6 relative z-10">The Mathematics of Growth: How Many Extra Calories?</h3>
      <p class="text-body mb-6 relative z-10 text-lg">
        One of the biggest and most destructive mistakes individuals make when attempting to gain mass is resorting to the infamous"dirty bulk". This archaic practice involves eating any type of food in abysmal quantities (ultra-processed foods, sugars, pizzas) completely ignoring nutrition. Although this guarantees a meteoric weight gain, the metabolic results are disastrous: insulin resistance, chronic lethargy, systemic inflammation, and a drastic increase in visceral adipose tissue, which is toxic to cardiovascular health.
      </p>
      <div class="bg-canvas-soft rounded-2xl p-6 border border-hairline mb-6 relative z-10">
        <ul class="space-y-5 text-body">
          <li class="flex items-start">
            <div class="bg-green-100 text-green-600 p-1.5 rounded-lg mr-4 mt-0.5 shrink-0">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"></path></svg>
            </div>
            <div>
              <strong class="text-ink block text-lg mb-1">For lean gains ("Lean Bulk"):</strong> 
              <span class="text-base text-body">Apply a conservative surplus of <strong>300 to 500 daily calories</strong> above your maintenance calories. This margin provides just the right amount of energy the body requires to efficiently synthesize new muscle tissue, almost completely limiting fat accumulation. The expected result is to gain between 1 and 1.5 kg (2-3 lbs) of solid weight per month.</span>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start">
            <div class="bg-surface text-brand p-1.5 rounded-lg mr-4 mt-0.5 shrink-0">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 10V3L4 14h7v7l9-11h-7z"></path></svg>
            </div>
            <div>
              <strong class="text-ink block text-lg mb-1">For the extreme"Hardgainer" or clinical underweight:</strong> 
              <span class="text-base text-body">If you possess an ultra-fast metabolism and a hyperactive NEAT, you might require an initial surplus of <strong>500 to 750 daily calories</strong>. However, this aggressive approach requires very rigorous monitoring and strenuous strength training to ensure the correct nutrient partitioning towards muscle cells instead of fat tissue.</span>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-brand-hover/80 font-medium bg-brand-soft p-4 rounded-xl relative z-10">
        📌 <strong>Critical Physiological Principle:</strong> The human body possesses a genetic and biological ceiling on the maximum amount of skeletal muscle it can build naturally (Muscle Protein Synthesis). A caloric surplus that vastly exceeds this synthesis limit will inevitably be stored as triglycerides in adipocytes (fat cells).
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-ink mt-16 mb-8 text-center">The Structural and Energetic Role of Macronutrients</h2>
    <p class="text-body leading-relaxed mb-10 text-center max-w-3xl mx-auto text-lg">
      If the total caloric surplus dictates <em>how much</em> weight you are going to gain, the precise proportion of <strong>macronutrients (proteins, carbohydrates, and fats)</strong> determines <em>what type</em> of tissue that weight will comprise, in addition to orchestrating your energy levels, strength, and hormonal regulation.
    </p>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-8 mb-16">
      <div class="bg-surface rounded-[2rem] p-8 border border-hairline shadow-[0_8px_30px_rgb(0,0,0,0.04)] hover:shadow-[0_8px_30px_rgb(0,0,0,0.12)] transition-all duration-300 transform hover:-translate-y-1">
        <div class="w-14 h-14 bg-gradient-to-br from-red-100 to-red-200 text-red-600 rounded-2xl flex items-center justify-center mb-6 shadow-sm">
          <svg class="w-7 h-7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M3 6l3 1m0 0l-3 9a5.002 5.002 0 006.001 0M6 7l3 9M6 7l6-2m6 2l3-1m-3 1l-3 9a5.002 5.002 0 006.001 0M18 7l3 9m-3-9l-6-2m0-2v2m0 16V5m0 16H9m3 0h3"></path></svg>
        </div>
        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-3">Proteins: The Building Material</h3>
        <p class="text-body leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Protein provides the essential amino acids necessary to repair and build micro-tears in muscle fibers after training. It is the mortar of your muscles.
        </p>
        <div class="bg-red-50 p-4 rounded-xl mt-auto">
          <p class="text-sm text-red-800 font-semibold mb-1">Scientific Target:</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-red-700">1.6 to 2.2 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight per day. Anything beyond this amount offers no additional proven anabolic benefits.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      
      <div class="bg-surface rounded-[2rem] p-8 border border-hairline shadow-[0_8px_30px_rgb(0,0,0,0.04)] hover:shadow-[0_8px_30px_rgb(0,0,0,0.12)] transition-all duration-300 transform hover:-translate-y-1">
        <div class="w-14 h-14 bg-gradient-to-br from-amber-100 to-amber-200 text-amber-600 rounded-2xl flex items-center justify-center mb-6 shadow-sm">
          <svg class="w-7 h-7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 10V3L4 14h7v7l9-11h-7z"></path></svg>
        </div>
        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-3">Carbohydrates: High Octane Fuel</h3>
        <p class="text-body leading-relaxed mb-4">
          They are the preferred fuel of the brain and active muscle. They fill glycogen stores and trigger the release of insulin, a powerful and anti-catabolic hormone.
        </p>
        <div class="bg-amber-50 p-4 rounded-xl mt-auto">
          <p class="text-sm text-amber-800 font-semibold mb-1">Scientific Target:</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">Make up 40% to 60% of your calories. Prioritize complex carbs (oats, rice, tubers) and simple carbs right before/after your workout.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-surface rounded-[2rem] p-8 border border-hairline shadow-[0_8px_30px_rgb(0,0,0,0.04)] hover:shadow-[0_8px_30px_rgb(0,0,0,0.12)] transition-all duration-300 transform hover:-translate-y-1">
        <div class="w-14 h-14 bg-gradient-to-br from-emerald-100 to-emerald-200 text-emerald-600 rounded-2xl flex items-center justify-center mb-6 shadow-sm">
          <svg class="w-7 h-7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19.428 15.428a2 2 0 00-1.022-.547l-2.387-.477a6 6 0 00-3.86.517l-.318.158a6 6 0 01-3.86.517L6.05 15.21a2 2 0 00-1.806.547M8 4h8l-1 1v5.172a2 2 0 00.586 1.414l5 5c1.26 1.26.367 3.414-1.415 3.414H4.828c-1.782 0-2.674-2.154-1.414-3.414l5-5A2 2 0 009 10.172V5L8 4z"></path></svg>
        </div>
        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-3">Fats: Density and Hormones</h3>
        <p class="text-body leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Providing 9 kcals/gram, they are the secret weapon to add calories without feeling suffocated by food. They are vital to synthesize testosterone and optimize endocrine health.
        </p>
        <div class="bg-emerald-50 p-4 rounded-xl mt-auto">
          <p class="text-sm text-emerald-800 font-semibold mb-1">Scientific Target:</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-emerald-700">0.8 to 1.2 grams per kilo of body weight, or 20-30% of your calories. Key sources: extra virgin olive oil, avocados, and nuts.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-ink mt-16 mb-8 text-center">The Definitive Roadmap: 5 Steps to Bulking</h2>
    
    <div class="space-y-6 md:space-y-12 relative before:absolute before:inset-0 before:ml-6 before:-translate-x-px md:before:mx-auto md:before:translate-x-0 before:h-full before:w-1 before:bg-gradient-to-b before:from-indigo-100 before:via-indigo-300 before:to-transparent mb-20 mt-10">
      
      <div class="relative flex items-center justify-between md:justify-normal md:odd:flex-row-reverse group">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center w-12 h-12 rounded-full border-4 border-white bg-brand text-white font-black text-xl shadow-lg shrink-0 md:order-1 md:group-odd:-translate-x-1/2 md:group-even:translate-x-1/2 z-10 transition-transform group-hover:scale-110">
          1
        </div>
        <div class="w-[calc(100%-4.5rem)] md:w-[calc(50%-3rem)] bg-surface p-8 rounded-3xl shadow-md border border-hairline hover:shadow-xl transition-all duration-300">
          <h4 class="font-extrabold text-xl text-ink mb-3">Calculate Your Baseline Maintenance (TDEE)</h4>
          <p class="text-body leading-relaxed">You can't plan a trip without knowing where you stand. Use accurate tools to calculate how many calories your basal metabolism burns added to your active lifestyle and gym routines. That exact number is your baseline or maintenance calories.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="relative flex items-center justify-between md:justify-normal md:odd:flex-row-reverse group">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center w-12 h-12 rounded-full border-4 border-white bg-brand text-white font-black text-xl shadow-lg shrink-0 md:order-1 md:group-odd:-translate-x-1/2 md:group-even:translate-x-1/2 z-10 transition-transform group-hover:scale-110">
          2
        </div>
        <div class="w-[calc(100%-4.5rem)] md:w-[calc(50%-3rem)] bg-surface p-8 rounded-3xl shadow-md border border-hairline hover:shadow-xl transition-all duration-300">
          <h4 class="font-extrabold text-xl text-ink mb-3">Structure an Architectural Surplus</h4>
          <p class="text-body leading-relaxed">To the number obtained in Step 1, strictly add 300 to 500 calories. No more, no less. Distribute this new total across your macros: first secure protein (1.8g/kg), then assign 25% to healthy fats, and fill the massive remaining gap exclusively with carbohydrates for explosive energy.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="relative flex items-center justify-between md:justify-normal md:odd:flex-row-reverse group">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center w-12 h-12 rounded-full border-4 border-white bg-brand text-white font-black text-xl shadow-lg shrink-0 md:order-1 md:group-odd:-translate-x-1/2 md:group-even:translate-x-1/2 z-10 transition-transform group-hover:scale-110">
          3
        </div>
        <div class="w-[calc(100%-4.5rem)] md:w-[calc(50%-3rem)] bg-surface p-8 rounded-3xl shadow-md border border-hairline hover:shadow-xl transition-all duration-300">
          <h4 class="font-extrabold text-xl text-ink mb-3">Mechanical Stimulus: Progressive Overload</h4>
          <p class="text-body leading-relaxed">Food doesn't create muscle by magic; it only provides the energy and materials. If you don't send a forceful signal to the body through demanding weightlifting (compound exercises like deadlifts, bench presses, and heavy squats), your body will happily store those extra 500 calories around your waistline. You must lift more weight or do more reps week after week.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="relative flex items-center justify-between md:justify-normal md:odd:flex-row-reverse group">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center w-12 h-12 rounded-full border-4 border-white bg-brand text-white font-black text-xl shadow-lg shrink-0 md:order-1 md:group-odd:-translate-x-1/2 md:group-even:translate-x-1/2 z-10 transition-transform group-hover:scale-110">
          4
        </div>
        <div class="w-[calc(100%-4.5rem)] md:w-[calc(50%-3rem)] bg-surface p-8 rounded-3xl shadow-md border border-hairline hover:shadow-xl transition-all duration-300">
          <h4 class="font-extrabold text-xl text-ink mb-3">The Sleep and Recovery Phase</h4>
          <p class="text-body leading-relaxed">Muscle doesn't grow in the gym. In the gym, you destroy fibers and catabolize tissue. Real growth (compensatory anabolism) happens while you sleep. You need 7 to 9 hours of deep, uninterrupted sleep. Without sleep, cortisol (the stress hormone) spikes, effectively blocking testosterone and growth hormone.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="relative flex items-center justify-between md:justify-normal md:odd:flex-row-reverse group">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center w-12 h-12 rounded-full border-4 border-white bg-brand text-white font-black text-xl shadow-lg shrink-0 md:order-1 md:group-odd:-translate-x-1/2 md:group-even:translate-x-1/2 z-10 transition-transform group-hover:scale-110">
          5
        </div>
        <div class="w-[calc(100%-4.5rem)] md:w-[calc(50%-3rem)] bg-surface p-8 rounded-3xl shadow-md border border-hairline hover:shadow-xl transition-all duration-300">
          <h4 class="font-extrabold text-xl text-ink mb-3">Meticulous Tracking and Micro-adjustments</h4>
          <p class="text-body leading-relaxed">The human metabolism adapts. As you gain weight, your body will burn more calories to maintain that new mass. Weigh yourself 3 times a week fasted and take an average. If after 15 days the average hasn't gone up by at least 400 grams, immediately increase your intake by another 200 calories. It is an iterative process of testing, measuring, and correcting.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-ink mt-16 mb-8">Appetite Biohacking: High-Density Tactics</h2>
    <p class="text-body leading-relaxed mb-8 text-lg">
      If you have a small stomach or fill up quickly, the prospect of chewing through 3,500 calories from rice, dry chicken, and broccoli turns out to be a tortuous task. Jaw fatigue and food burnout ruin bulking phases. To bypass the gastric satiety block, nutritional science offers potent"shortcuts" or caloric density optimizations.
    </p>

    <div class="bg-canvas-soft rounded-[2.5rem] p-8 md:p-12 mb-12 border border-hairline">
      <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-6 flex items-center">
        <span class="bg-surface text-brand-hover p-2 rounded-xl mr-4"><svg class="w-6 h-6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 11H5m14 0a2 2 0 012 2v6a2 2 0 01-2 2H5a2 2 0 01-2-2v-6a2 2 0 012-2m14 0V9a2 2 0 00-2-2M5 11V9a2 2 0 002-2m0 0V5a2 2 0 012-2h6a2 2 0 012 2v2M7 7h10"></path></svg></span>
        Tactic 1: Drink Your Calories (Liquid Calories)
      </h3>
      <p class="text-body mb-6 text-lg">
        The human digestive system and gastric stretch receptors react with great intensity to solid foods, sending strong"I'm full" nerve signals to the hypothalamus. However, blended foods quickly bypass the gastrointestinal tract without triggering severe satiety responses. Crafting dense shakes is the cornerstone of hardgainer success.
      </p>
      
      <div class="bg-surface p-8 rounded-3xl border border-hairline mb-8 shadow-sm">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-ink mb-4">The Master Formula for the Anabolic Shake (1,150 Kcal):</h4>
        <ul class="space-y-3 text-body text-lg">
          <li class="flex items-center"><svg class="w-5 h-5 text-green-500 mr-3 shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"></path></svg> 400 ml of Whole Milk or Oat Milk (high in energy).</li>
          <li class="flex items-center"><svg class="w-5 h-5 text-green-500 mr-3 shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"></path></svg> 2 scoops of high-quality Whey Protein.</li>
          <li class="flex items-center"><svg class="w-5 h-5 text-green-500 mr-3 shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"></path></svg> 120 grams of finely blended Oats (complex carbohydrates).</li>
          <li class="flex items-center"><svg class="w-5 h-5 text-green-500 mr-3 shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"></path></svg> 2 heaping tablespoons (40g) of pure Peanut Butter.</li>
          <li class="flex items-center"><svg class="w-5 h-5 text-green-500 mr-3 shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"></path></svg> 1 large ripe Banana.</li>
          <li class="flex items-center"><svg class="w-5 h-5 text-green-500 mr-3 shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"></path></svg> 15 ml (one tablespoon) of Extra Virgin Olive Oil (imperceptible to taste, adds +130 pure kcal).</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      
      <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-6 flex items-center mt-12">
        <span class="bg-amber-100 text-amber-700 p-2 rounded-xl mr-4"><svg class="w-6 h-6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M8 7h12m0 0l-4-4m4 4l-4 4m0 6H4m0 0l4 4m-4-4l4-4"></path></svg></span>
        Tactic 2: Smart Food Matrix Swaps
      </h3>
      <p class="text-body mb-6 text-lg">
        Food selection is imperative. Foods packed with water or extreme amounts of fiber take up immense gastric volume while providing negligible calories. This is called low energy density. You must shift the spectrum towards nutritionally dense foods.
      </p>
      
      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-3xl border border-hairline shadow-sm mt-8 bg-surface">
        <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse min-w-[700px]">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-surface-elevated text-white text-lg">
              <th class="p-5 font-semibold w-1/3">Satiating Food (Avoid in excess)</th>
              <th class="p-5 font-semibold w-1/3 text-indigo-200">High-Density Swap</th>
              <th class="p-5 font-semibold w-1/3">Thermodynamic Impact</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-base text-body divide-y divide-gray-100">
            <tr class="hover:bg-canvas-soft transition-colors">
              <td class="p-5 font-medium">Chicken Breast (Dry, boiled)</td>
              <td class="p-5 font-bold text-brand-hover">Roasted Chicken Thighs (with skin)</td>
              <td class="p-5 text-body">+ 150 kcal per identical portion, superior lipid profile for hormones.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="hover:bg-canvas-soft transition-colors">
              <td class="p-5 font-medium">Baked Potato or Cauliflower Rice</td>
              <td class="p-5 font-bold text-brand-hover">Egg Pasta or Jasmine Rice</td>
              <td class="p-5 text-body">Less insoluble fiber, hyper-fast digestion, +200 kcal easily assimilated.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="hover:bg-canvas-soft transition-colors">
              <td class="p-5 font-medium">Skim Milk or Water</td>
              <td class="p-5 font-bold text-brand-hover">Whole Milk or Natural Grape Juice</td>
              <td class="p-5 text-body">+ 70 kcal per glass, without altering satiety levels whatsoever.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="hover:bg-canvas-soft transition-colors">
              <td class="p-5 font-medium">Apples, Watermelon, Strawberries</td>
              <td class="p-5 font-bold text-brand-hover">Raisins, Dates, Dried Fruit</td>
              <td class="p-5 text-body">By extracting water, you concentrate the natural sugar. Easy to eat 400 kcal.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-ink mt-16 mb-8">The Dark Side: Myths and Risks of Gaining Weight</h2>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 mb-16">
      <div class="bg-surface p-8 rounded-3xl border border-hairline shadow-lg relative overflow-hidden group">
        <div class="absolute top-0 left-0 w-2 h-full bg-red-500 group-hover:w-full group-hover:opacity-5 transition-all duration-500"></div>
        <div class="mb-4 text-red-500">
